WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:10.620
The TSOE OMVS command will allow users to start more than one session with the shell.

2
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.940
Only one session is displayed at a time on the screen.

3
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.600
The multi-session support in the shell allows these shell processes to reside as tasks

4
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.760
in the same address space.

5
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.680
The user can switch between different sessions using a PF key.

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.380
The following are OMVS sub-commands to control the multi-session support.

7
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.200
Those are open, which starts a new shell session.

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.300
Close or quit, closes a shell session.

9
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.560
Quit all, ends all sessions and returns to TSOE.

10
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.420
Next sesh, switches to the next session and previous sesh, switches to the previous session.

11
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.760
The non-TSOE user can log into multiple shell sessions using the same user ID and password.

12
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.960
You can also be logged onto the shell using the TSOE interface and the R login interface

13
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.120
using the same user ID and password.

14
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.140
So here's an example of a shell script with some logic.

15
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.960
This example has a for loop, for do, done.

16
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.900
It also has an if condition that runs a sequence of commands if a particular condition is met.

17
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.020
If then fi.

18
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.540
This example of a shell script takes one positional parameter, giving the name of a file.

19
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.920
The script looks in the working directory and finds the names of all .c files.

20
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.520
The if control structure inside the for loop tests each file to see if it's older than

21
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.740
the file named on the command line.

22
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:44.860
If the .c file is older, echo displays the name and the file is compiled.

23
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.440
So what we've seen there is a shell script that has the capability to run a sequence

24
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.080
of commands stored in a text file.

25
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.760
The commands in the file can be run by entering the file name on the command line.

26
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:04.680
After a shell script is created, a user needs read and execute permission to run the script.

27
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.540
A shell is like a program made up of shell commands.

28
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:13.360
The shell supports many of the features of regular programming languages like variables,

29
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.340
control structures and functions.

30
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:23.340
A set of zosunix extensions to TSOE Rex provides the capability for Rex execs to access zosunix

31
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.440
callable services.

32
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.680
The zosunix extensions to Rex are called syscall commands and they have names that correspond

33
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:36.600
to the names of the callable services they invoke, for example exec, fork and pipe.

34
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:43.340
Rex execs using zosunix extensions can run from TSOE or in zosbatch and access the zosunix

35
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:50.060
functions and services through a host command environment rather than discrete external functions.

36
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.940
The host command environment is called syscall.

37
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:59.020
The environment must be initialized by a syscall's on statement in the beginning of the exec.

38
00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:04.480
Rex execs can also be run in the zosunix shell or from a C program using the exec system

39
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.780
call.

40
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.940
This is different from shell scripts which are only interpreted in the shell.

41
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.820
This also means that shell scripts can invoke a Rex exec just like they would invoke any

42
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.660
program.

43
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.000
When running a Rex exec in the shell or from a C program, the syscall environment is

44
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.030
automatically initialized.

45
00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:31.350
There are many ways in zosunix to send a message to another user.

46
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.910
Which method to use depends on the requirements and the timeframes you want the message to

47
00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:41.030
be sent, such as logger and write.

48
00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:46.990
As a TSO user, you might prefer to just use facilities provided by TSO instead.

49
00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:52.830
The clock daemon cron is a facility that allows you to run a command at a specific time, run

50
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:58.090
a command in batch when the system activity is low, or set up commands, scripts or programs

51
00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:02.190
to run at the same time every day or every week.

52
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.630
Batch work is assigned bottom priority.

53
00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:10.410
The cron daemon examines the at, batch or crontab files at initialization or whenever

54
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.870
they change and start the functions at the specified times.

55
00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.390
It uses the timezone in effect when the command is executed.

56
00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:25.030
Therefore, be sure to set the TZ environment variable before starting cron.

57
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.750
In the next video, we'll look at BPX batch.
